      {
        "title": "Complex logarithm series",
        "body": "For $|z|=1$, $z\\ne1$, $\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\frac{z^n}n=-\\ln(1-z)$ (convergent by Dirichlet's test and Abel's theorem). With $z=e^{i}$: $\\sum\\frac{\\cos n}n=\\operatorname{Re}(-\\ln(1-e^i))=-\\ln|1-e^i|=-\\tfrac12\\ln(2-2\\cos1)$. And $\\sum\\frac{\\sin n}n=\\operatorname{Im}(-\\ln(1-e^i))=-\\arg(1-e^i)$. Now $1-e^{i}=-2i\\sin\\frac12\\,e^{i/2}=2\\sin\\frac12\\,e^{i(\\frac12-\\frac\\pi2)}$, so $\\arg(1-e^i)=\\frac{1-\\pi}2$ and $\\sum\\frac{\\sin n}n=\\frac{\\pi-1}2$. Adding gives $\\frac{\\pi-1}2-\\frac12\\ln(2-2\\cos1)$ ($\\approx1.1128$).\n\n**Answer: $\\frac{\\pi-1}{2}-\\frac12\\ln(2-2\\cos 1)$**"
      }
